Package: mos-system
Version: @VERSION@
Architecture: @ARCH@
Maintainer: mos build <mos@example.invalid>
Section: admin
Priority: optional
Depends: systemd, systemd-sysv, systemd-resolved, systemd-repart, udev, dbus, kmod, openssh-server, iproute2, libubootenv-tool, curl, passwd
Description: mos common system policy, state mounts and operator account
 The board-independent half of a mos root filesystem: the seed and reconcile
 tools under /usr/lib/mos, their units, the STATE and DATA bind mounts
 (/var/lib/mos, /etc/ssh, /etc/hostname, /home, /root,
 /usr/local/lib/systemd/system), the six board-independent repart.d position
 holders, the /var fill-up policy, the image's default DHCP network and
 journald's volatile storage policy.
 .
 It owns the /var/lib/mos mountpoint. That directory and var-lib-mos.mount are
 two halves of one mechanism -- mosd.service declares
 RequiresMountsFor=/var/lib/mos -- so mosd names this package in Depends
 rather than shipping the directory itself.
 .
 It ships the preset that keeps sshd disabled on both profiles, because mosd
 owns that lifecycle: openssh-server's own postinst would otherwise enable
 ssh.service, and openssh-server is in Depends above partly so that dpkg unpacks
 this preset before it is configured. The postinst asserts that outcome; it does
 not arrange it.
 .
 The postinst creates the pinned mos operator account, uid and gid 1000, home
 /home/mos, shell /bin/bash. The uid is written out rather than allocated
 because /home/mos lives on the DATA partition and survives every A/B update,
 so its owner is part of the on-disk contract. The account is granted no
 supplementary groups and no sudo, and it is not a lesser privilege level:
 sshd authorises root and mos from one key list.
 .
 Architecture: all, and the Depends above asks for no shlibs substitution.
 Nothing in the payload is an ELF, so there is no shared-library dependency
 for the packer to resolve -- asking would be an error rather than an empty
 expansion. Every entry above is a relationship ELF metadata could not show
 anyway: systemd-repart is a separate package on trixie and its absence is
 silent -- DATA simply never grows; libubootenv-tool provides fw_printenv for
 the machine-id oneshot; curl is what the health gate probes apid with, and
 without it that probe SKIPs and the gate reports green over two of three
 components; passwd provides the useradd, groupadd and chage the postinst
 execs by name.
 .
 RAUC's runtime libraries are deliberately not here. glib, json-glib and
 libfdisk are in the image for the self-built rauc alone and belong to
 mos-rauc; they are installed by the base stage today only because the stage
 chain had nowhere else to put them.
